    "body": "BlueGrace Logistics announced it will change private equity hands. The managed logistics provider said middle-market investor Littlejohn & Co. will replace Warburg Pincus as its primary growth partner.\nFinancial terms of the transaction were not disclosed. The deal is expected to close by the end of the year.\nTampa, Florida-based BlueGrace serves more than 10,000 customers out of offices in the U.S. and Mexico. The deal will include additional investment in its BlueShip platform, which has over 250,000 carriers, providing less-than-truckload, truckload, reefer and multimodal transportation services. The transaction will also help BlueGrace expand its logistics staff and account teams.\n“BlueGrace is growing, and this gives us a partner who shares our long-term view to keep doing what’s working, investing in our people, in BlueShip, and in the service our customers and carriers count on,” said Bobby Harris, founder and CEO of BlueGrace Logistics, in a news release.\nThe company will continue to be led by the current leadership team, including Harris.\nThe release didn’t mention future M&A, but BlueGrace has been acquisitive over the past two years. It acquired Idaho-based 3PL Truk TMS in August. Last year, it acquired Michigan-based 3PL STB Freight Group and Florida-based broker FreightCenter .\n“Warburg Pincus has been a strong partner through this stage of our growth, and I’m grateful for their support,” Harris said. “With Littlejohn’s backing, we’re building for the long run.”\nEvercore is serving as financial adviser to BlueGrace, with J.P. Morgan advising Littlejohn.\nWhy it matters? BlueGrace’s transition to a new private equity partner and track record of acquisitions highlight robust capital availability and aggressive expansion within the 3PL sector. Additionally, ongoing investments in proprietary platforms like BlueShip emphasize the need for scaled digital capabilities to remain competitive.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nJ.B. Hunt sees cost pressures ease, touts dedicated wins\nBroker RXO says TL spot rates not entering deflationary phase\nOctober TL rates surge as shipment counts extend positive streak",
